# Listen

WebRTC has no conventional host-and-port listener. Its accepting half is a
signaling exchange. `@glowcap/webrtc` currently exposes the dialing connector;
applications that answer offers drive the peer and signaling objects directly.
The native Zig and C companions expose the same lower-level accepting pieces,
but no binding currently wraps them as an application-level `Server`.

# The channel comes up by signaling

To open a DataChannel, the two peers first trade three things: an SDP offer, an
SDP answer, and a handful of ICE candidates (the network paths each side can be
reached on). glowcap carries that exchange over an ordinary OCapN capability. A
room both peers have joined, or any object both can send to, hosts the
signaling. The object moves opaque strings; it parses no SDP and holds no
session.

[The two peers trade an offer, an answer, and ICE candidates through a signaling capability, then open a direct DataChannel and run the keyed session over it with the signaling host out of the byte path.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-webrtc-signaling.svg'))

A bare `webrtc` locator does not dial on its own. glowcap recognizes the
transport and checks the designator (a public key), but with no signaling
reference there is nowhere to send an offer. TypeScript puts signaling on the
Host. Native Zig puts it on the WebRTC backend. Once it is in place, the dial
adopts the DataChannel. Without signaling the dial refuses.

# One key, pinned by the handshake

Like keyed TCP and iroh, a WebRTC peer is named by its Ed25519 public key in
canonical hex, not by an address. Signaling brings the channel up, and then the
keyed handshake pins the session to that designator. Whoever answers has to
prove the key the peer was introduced under, so a signaling host that swapped
in a peer of its own is refused before the session ever opens. The DTLS
fingerprint is trusted to introduce, never to impersonate, because it cannot
complete the keyed handshake for a key it does not hold.

# Optional sidecar archive

glowcap's WebRTC DataChannel sidecar is Rust, so it rides a staticlib in
`libglowcap_net_transport_webrtc`
([ADR-0089](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0089-webrtc-datachannel-netlayer.md)).
The base archives stay pure Zig; a program links WebRTC only when it wants it.
Building the companion archive needs `cargo`.

A native endpoint exposes one readiness descriptor and stable peer ids for many
DataChannels. The one-session blocking connection remains available. The scale
path adopts those peers into the same Host loop iroh uses, with dials driven by
a configured signaling authority.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) says which WebRTC checks run
in CI and which are still manual.

# The browser arm

`@glowcap/webrtc` drives an `RTCPeerConnection` and presents the open
DataChannel to the wasm Host. It supports two topologies:

- a browser and a native peer hold a keyed session over a DataChannel, and
- two browsers, introduced by a signaling host, bring up a direct DataChannel
  and hold a keyed session with the host out of the byte path.

# STUN, TURN, and staying direct

ICE finds a network path between the peers. The `@glowcap/webrtc` connector
adds no servers unless the application configures some, and a relay is only a
fallback, never the normal path.

- On loopback or a LAN, peers connect directly on host candidates, with no
  STUN or TURN.
- A browser that must cross a NAT gives the connector a STUN server. Rooms
  does this by default, using Cloudflare's public STUN service, and serves the
  same configuration to both browsers. Setting it explicitly to empty keeps the
  page free of outside servers.
- A Rooms deployment can also keep a TURN issuer token on its server and give
  each new browser connection short-lived TURN credentials.
- The native sidecar gathers only host candidates, so a native peer connects
  on loopback or a LAN.

A TURN relay carries bytes only when ICE finds no direct path. Even then it
sits under DTLS, so it sees timing and volume but never the keyed session.

# Three-party handoff over WebRTC

A handoff over WebRTC is the same introduction as over any other session
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the exchange): the gifter names the
exporter, and the recipient redeems by dialing that peer on a fresh session.
glowcap redeems that path when the redeeming host has signaling authority
configured. Without that authority, a bare `webrtc` locator still refuses
because it has no route for the signaling exchange.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) owns the evidence boundary.

For native peers that need discovery or NAT traversal, prefer
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')). The rest of the lineup is on
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')).
